    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou.jpg" alt="King Wu of Zhou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Wu_of_Zhou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ma Lin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Qi was founded shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> conquest of the Shang, conventionally dated to 1046 BCE. According to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a></em>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> enfeoffed his leading general and adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-ziya">Jiang Ziya</a>, known as Lord Taigong, with land in the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> peninsula, where he established his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingqiu">Yingqiu</a>, traditionally identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>. The fief was a frontier outpost among the Dongyi, peoples of the east who resisted Zhou rule and joined the rebellions that followed King Wu's death. Tradition says that Taigong simplified rites and adapted to local customs, encouraged crafts and exploited the fish and salt of the coast, so that people and merchants flocked to the state. Its rulers, of the Jiang surname, were not kin of the royal Ji clan but were tied to it by repeated marriages, and Qi princesses became queens of Zhou. In its early centuries the state was a middle-ranking power whose relations with the court were not always easy: one ruler, Duke Ai, was reportedly boiled to death at the Zhou court in the ninth century BCE. Qi expanded by absorbing small neighbouring polities, such as the state of Ji, which it annexed in 690 BCE, and by the early seventh century BCE it was the dominant power of the east.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tian.jpg" alt="Tian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A9-bronze-shang.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Digital file creation, see the contributor names shown in the “User” column of the “File history” section below., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Qi was one of the richest states of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its coastline supplied salt and fish, its plains grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and wheat, and the region was famed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and fine textiles; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> wrote that Qi supplied caps, sashes, clothes and shoes to the whole world. Under Duke Huan the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> is credited with organising state control of salt and iron, measures later attributed to him in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzi">Guanzi</a></em>, a collection of texts largely written by later Qi scholars. Qi cast its own bronze knife-shaped money, the <em>daobi</em>, which circulated widely in the east. Its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>, became perhaps the largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>: a speech recorded in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhanguo-ce">Zhanguo ce</a></em> credits it with 70,000 households and describes streets so crowded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> hubs knocked together, and inhabitants who played music, raced dogs and played the board game <em>liubo</em>. Excavations show a walled city of about fifteen square kilometres with workshops for bronze, iron, bone and coins. Linzi was also an intellectual centre. From the fourth century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> rulers supported scholars at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jixia-academy">Jixia Academy</a> near the city's Ji Gate with stipends and houses; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, Xunzi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zou-yan">Zou Yan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-dao">Shen Dao</a> and Tian Pian were among those associated with it. The magicians known as <em>fangshi</em>, who sought immortality on islands in the eastern sea, also flourished in Qi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-an"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-an.jpg" alt="Battle of An" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-an">Battle of An</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huahill_2008_09_14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rolfmueller, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Qi's rise as a military power began under Duke Huan (r. 685–643 BCE), who used his wealth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a>'s reorganisation of the population into military districts to field large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> armies. As hegemon, or <em>ba</em>, he led coalitions in defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> order: he helped the State of Yan against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanrong">Shanrong</a> in 663 BCE, and in 656 BCE marched with allied lords against the southern State of Chu, which agreed to a covenant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenant-of-shaoling">Shaoling</a>. After Huan's death Qi lost primacy to the State of Jin, which defeated it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-an">Battle of An</a> in 589 BCE. Qi is associated with famous military thinkers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Wu</a>, the traditional author of <em>The Art of War</em>, was said to come from Qi, and his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-bin">Sun Bin</a>, as strategist for the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Ji, defeated the State of Wei at the Battle of Guiling in 354 or 353 BCE and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maling">Battle of Maling</a> in 342 BCE, where the Wei commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pang-juan">Pang Juan</a> died. Its greatest disaster came in 284 BCE, when an allied army under the Yan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-yi">Yue Yi</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a> and more than seventy cities, leaving only Ju and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimo">Jimo</a>; Tian Dan broke the siege of Jimo with a stampede of oxen carrying burning reeds and recovered the state by 279 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guan-zhong.jpg" alt="Guan Zhong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qihuangongguanzhong2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Qi was ruled first by the Jiang lineage descended from Taigong, and from the fourth century BCE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> lineage, who took the title of king. The administration associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a>, minister to Duke Huan, divided the capital region into districts that combined civilian registration with military service, although the detailed accounts in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guoyu">Guoyu</a></em> and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzi">Guanzi</a></em> were written later and idealised. Powerful ministerial families dominated the court in the sixth century BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yan-ying">Yan Ying</a> served Duke Jing (r. 547–490 BCE) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> visited the state. The Tian, descended from Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-wan">Wan of the State of Chen</a>, who had fled to Qi in 672 BCE, won popularity by lending grain with large measures and collecting it with small ones. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-chang">Tian Chang</a> killed Duke Jian in 481 BCE and dominated his successors, and in 386 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-an-of-zhou">King An of Zhou</a> recognised Tian He as ruler of Qi; the last Jiang ruler died in 379 BCE. Under the Tian, Qi became a centralised territorial state, organised into five major districts (<em>du</em>) with their own garrisons and governed by officials appointed by the ruler. King Wei (r. c. 356–320 BCE) is remembered for disciplining officials by rewarding the honest governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimo">Jimo</a> and boiling the corrupt governor of E, and in 334 BCE he and King Hui of Wei recognised each other as kings at Xuzhou.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/analects"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/analects.jpg" alt="Analects" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rongo_Analects_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Confucius and his disciples, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Qi's influence on Chinese thought was greater than its military record. Duke Huan's hegemony created a model in which a powerful lord protected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> order and the Chinese states against outside peoples, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> for it, saying in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a></em> that without him people would be wearing their hair loose and folding their robes to the left. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzi">Guanzi</a></em>, attributed to Guan Zhong but compiled from the writings of later Qi scholars, became a foundational text of statecraft and economic thought and informed later debates on state monopolies of salt and iron under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jixia-academy">Jixia Academy</a> was the first known institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> where a state supported scholars of rival schools to teach and debate; it helped shape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> and Xunzi, and the cosmology of yin and yang and the Five Phases through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zou-yan">Zou Yan</a>, ideas that became central to Han thought. Qi traditions of sacrifice at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a> and of the quest for immortality later drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">First Emperor of Qin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaogong-ji">Kaogong ji</a></em>, a technical treatise on crafts preserved in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a></em>, is often thought to come from Qi. After unification the name Qi survived as a kingdom title under the Han and as a dynastic name in the fifth and sixth centuries CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin.jpg" alt="King Zhaoxiang of Qin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin">King Zhaoxiang of Qin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heshibi_%E5%AE%8C%E7%92%A7%E5%BD%92%E8%B5%B5%E6%AD%A6%E6%B0%8F%E7%A5%A0_%E9%87%91%E7%9F%B3%E7%B4%A23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ChenDaoIsHere, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Qi's last great expansion proved fatal. King Xuan exploited a succession crisis in Yan to occupy it in 314 BCE but was forced to withdraw, leaving Yan bent on revenge. King Min (r. c. 300–284 BCE) briefly took the title of Eastern Emperor alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin">King Zhao of Qin</a> in 288 BCE, and in 286 BCE annexed the wealthy State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, alarming every neighbour. In 284 BCE Yan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Zhao, Wei and Han attacked together under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-yi">Yue Yi</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a> was sacked, its treasures carried to Yan, and King Min fled to Ju, where he was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nao-chi">Nao Chi</a>, a general sent by Chu. Although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Dan restored the state under King Xiang by 279 BCE, Qi never regained its former strength. In the last decades of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> its rulers, King Jian and his mother, Queen Dowager Jun, kept friendly relations with Qin and stayed out of the wars in which Qin destroyed Han, Zhao, Wei, Chu and Yan one by one, a policy encouraged by the minister Hou Sheng, who was reportedly bribed by Qin. When Qin finally turned on Qi in 221 BCE, its general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-ben">Wang Ben</a> entered Linzi from the north, and King Jian surrendered without a battle. The fall of Qi completed the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">King Zheng of Qin</a>, who then took the title of First Emperor.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Qi was the great eastern state of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for eight centuries: under Duke Huan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> it created the first hegemony over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> states, its salt, iron and textile wealth made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a> one of the largest cities of its age, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jixia-academy">Jixia Academy</a> shaped classical Chinese philosophy.</em></p>

